## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to engineer a practical, scalable evaluation system for real-world multilingual policy assistants operating under complex brand-specific constraints with live documentation dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual policy alignment across distinct brand ecosystems
- Versioned, source-grounded citation verification and tracking
- Cross-brand policy conflict detection and temporal drift analysis
- Lightweight evaluation datasets with granular per-claim scoring mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example citation validation function
function validateCitationCurrentness(claim, policyIndex) {
  const citation = claim?.citation;
  const policy = policyIndex.find(p => p.id === citation?.policyId);
  return policy && policy.version === citation?.version && policy.isActive;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

To assess the ability to design policy-aware, scalable agent orchestration that handles multi-tenant data isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration with AutoGen planning
- Private APIs and data catalog integration
- Policy enforcement and tenant isolation
- End-to-end traceability, auditing, and failure handling
- Resilience patterns: retries, backoff, circuit breakers

## Code Example

```javascript
async function plan(request) {
  const { userId, tenantId, dataScope } = request;

  const policyResult = await policyEngine.check({ tenantId, dataScope });
  if (!policyResult.allowed) {
    throw new Error('Access denied: policy violation');
  }

  const catalogResult = await dataCatalog.query({ tenantId, dataScope });
  const apiResult = await privateApi.fetch({ tenantId, dataScope });

  await auditLogger.log({
    tenantId,
    userId,
    steps: [policyResult, catalogResult, apiResult],
    traceId: uuid()
  });

  return { data: apiResult, traceId: traceId };
}
```

This flow ensures tenant-scoped policies are enforced at entry, each component call is traced, and failures are handled with compensating transactions or graceful degradation.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to implement a minimal retrieval loop that works with embeddings, chunking, and a vector-like store, while considering multilingual routing and update freshness.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory vector store
- Text chunking with overlap
- Embedding generation
- Cosine similarity search
- Multilingual handling
- Update freshness and caching

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS snippet: minimal in-memory index
const model = loadModel('all-MiniLM-L6-v2');
function chunk(text, max=300, overlap=50){ /* split and overlap */ }
let index = [];
function indexDocs(docs){
  docs.forEach(doc=>{
    chunk(doc.text).forEach(ch=>{
      index.push({id: doc.id+':' + hash(ch), text: ch, emb: model.encode(ch)});
    });
  });
}
function query(q, topK=5){
  const qEmb = model.encode(q);
  return index
    .map(n => ({...n, score: cosine(n.emb, qEmb)}))
    .sort((a,b)=> b.score-a.score)
    .slice(0, topK);
}
```

